      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve for \\(\\mathbf{A}^{100}\\), we first need to understand the behavior of the matrix \\(\\mathbf{A}\\) when raised to successive powers. The matrix \\(\\mathbf{A}\\) is a permutation matrix that cyclically permutes the indices 1, 2, and 3. Specifically, applying \\(\\mathbf{A}\\) three times returns the vector to its original order:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^3 = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}^3 = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\mathbf{A}.\n\\]\n\nThis shows that \\(\\mathbf{A}^3 = \\mathbf{I}\\), where \\(\\mathbf{I}\\) is the \\(3 \\times 3\\) identity matrix. From this, we can deduce that:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^{3k} = (\\mathbf{A}^3)^k = \\mathbf{I}^k = \\mathbf{I} \\quad \\text{for any integer } k.\n\\]\n\nThus, \\(\\mathbf{A}^{100}\\) can be written as \\(\\mathbf{A}^{3 \\cdot 33 + 1}\\), and since \\(\\mathbf{A}^{3k} = \\mathbf{I}\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^{100} = (\\mathbf{A}^3)^{33} \\cdot \\mathbf{A} = \\mathbf{I}^{33} \\cdot \\mathbf{A} = \\mathbf{I} \\cdot \\mathbf{A} = \\mathbf{A}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(\\mathbf{A}^{100}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}}.\n\\]",
