      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "The matrix $\\mathbf{A}$ is a special type of matrix known as a permutation matrix. Such matrices permute the rows and columns in a circular manner. The powers of $\\mathbf{A}$ form a cycle. Let's compute the first few powers to identify the pattern:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^1 = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^2 = \\mathbf{A} \\cdot \\mathbf{A} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\mathbf{I}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^3 = \\mathbf{A}^2 \\cdot \\mathbf{A} = \\mathbf{I} \\cdot \\mathbf{A} = \\mathbf{A}\n\\]\n\nWe observe that $\\mathbf{A}^2 = \\mathbf{I}$, so every power of $\\mathbf{A}$ beyond $\\mathbf{A}$ will be the identity matrix $\\mathbf{I}$. Therefore, for any integer $n$:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^{100} = (\\mathbf{A}^2)^{50} = \\mathbf{I}^{50} = \\mathbf{I}\n\\]\n\nThus, the answer is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]",
